WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.940
When Luke Skywalker became one with the Force
at the end of Star Wars: The Last Jedi, fans

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.410
were left sobbing and sniffling, weeping and
wondering whether they'd ever see the legendary

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.210
Jedi on the silver screen again.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:16.740
Sadly, we now know that Luke truly has passed
and thanks to actor Mark Hamill, we know what

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:17.720
really killed him.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.400
Taking to Twitter on Monday, October 22nd,
Hamill shared two images from the comic book

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:27.200
adaptation of The Last Jedi specifically the
panels that illustrate Luke's death on the

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.260
the island planet Ahch-To.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.620
Both the film and the comic adaptation depict
Luke's passing as somewhat tranquil, but Hamill

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.000
has a completely different idea of what went
down.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.520
The actor revealed that the Force actually
killed Luke, likening him to a drug addict

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.820
who relapsed after years of sobriety and ultimately
overdosed.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.580
"THE FORCE KILLED LUKE.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.240
You have to acknowledge the irony in his fate.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.979
Almost like an addict that kicked his habit
cold-turkey, remained clean for decades, only

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.220
to re-use just once &amp; then, tragically, overdoses.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.979
As those who have seen The Last Jedi will
remember, Luke vanishes into thin air — leaving

00:01:00.979 --> 00:01:05.320
nothing but his cloak behind — after he
reconnects with the Force and creates a projection

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.850
of himself in an effort to protect his twin
sister Leia and save the few surviving members

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.540
of the Resistance from Kylo Ren and the First
Order.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.799
Luke's act of bravery was successful, as he
engages in an intense duel with Kylo in front

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.040
of the abandoned Rebel Alliance base on the
small, remote mineral planet of Crait.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.680
When Kylo swings his crossguard lightsaber
at Luke, he realizes that he isn't battling

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.040
a physical Jedi but rather, he's fighting
against a Force projection.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.700
The camera then shifts to show Luke dying
on Ahch-To, staring into the setting sun.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.280
That's quite a stark contrast compared to
Hamill's report of the circumstances surrounding

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:40.920
Luke's death.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.460
On the surface, Luke's passing wasn't all
sunshine and Alderaanian flame-lilies and

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:50.259
goblets of blue alien milk, of course, but
it did feel markedly serene as if the scene

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:54.619
was implying that Luke was passing from one
realm to another, moving from completing a

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.320
physical journey to embarking on a spiritual
one.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.220
According to Hamill, however, there was turmoil
and pain bubbling underneath, as Luke was

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.520
unable to shield himself from the corruptive
side of the Force.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.819
Hamill's take on Luke's death is interesting
for sure, as it suggests that the Force is

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.260
something addictive and lethal to even the
strongest of minds.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.340
Though he's gone from the land of the living,
Hamill's Luke isn't entirely missing from

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.220
the galaxy far, far away.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:24.460
The actor previously confirmed that Luke will
appear in director J.J. Abrams' Episode Nine,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.300
due out on December 21, 2019.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.680
Fans have theorized that he'll circle back
either in flashback sequences or as a Force

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.519
ghost like Anakin Skywalker, Yoda, and Obi-Wan
Kenobi have all done in the past to continue

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:42.180
training Rey in the ways of the Force and
perhaps teach her one final, infinitely valuable

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:42.680
lesson.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.020
Some have even speculated that Luke will return
to haunt Kylo Ren, which would admittedly

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.560
be pretty hilarious to see.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.180
But while Hamill clearly isn't shy about talking
all things Star Wars after films have been

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.520
in theaters for a while, he's not in the business
of addressing theories ahead of a movie's

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.020
release, so fans shouldn't hold their breath
for him to confirm or deny any rumors.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.800
In mid-October, 2018, Hamill tweeted

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.000
"I mention keeping my #StarWars opinions to
myself &amp; what happens?

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.769
It triggers an avalanche of questions about
the next one! [...] yes, I'm in it.

00:03:12.769 --> 00:03:15.959
Other than that, just consider it: EPISODE
NEIN [ COMMENT."

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.970
So the manner in which Luke will return for
Episode Nine remains a total mystery.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.720
But there are two things of which we're absolutely
certain: don't ask Mark Hamill about anything

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.530
related to the upcoming Skywalker saga installment
and don't even think about dabbling in using

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.060
the Force.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:31.620
It's too dangerous, kids.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:33.760
You don't want to end up like Luke, do you?

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:34.590
Didn't think so.

